<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  fix/track-list-polish (l): a mouse-wheel scroll must be able to bring the
  TRUE LAST track fully into view.

  THE BUG. Vertical scrolling is row-granular: SStdMixerView::rowCount()-vis
  used to be the whole story for the scrollbar's `maximum()`, where `vis` is
  SStdMixerView::visibleRowCountFrom() - "how many rows are visible from a
  given top row". That function counts a row that only PARTIALLY fits inside
  the viewport as fully "visible" (its loop stops the moment the remaining
  height goes non-positive, AFTER charging that row's full height), so
  whenever the total row height does not divide evenly into the viewport
  height, the computed maximum can land exactly at the content's own bottom
  edge - with no scroll room left to bring the true last row's remaining
  pixels into view. Six 100px rows (600px of content) in a viewport whose
  lane band is not a multiple of 100px hits exactly this: the counting loop
  consumes the whole viewport DURING the sixth row, so `vis` comes out equal
  to `rowCount`, and the old `maximum = rowCount - vis` is 0 - no scrolling
  at all, even though the sixth row is only partially inside the viewport.

  THE FIX reserves roughly one track's height of extra scroll headroom
  whenever the real content is taller than the viewport (SStdMixerView::
  verticalScrollMaximum(), used by both recalcPageStep() and
  nTracksChanged() so a track add/remove cannot silently undo it). This is
  scroll RANGE only: no row is added, nothing is drawn, and it is not a drop
  target (that is item (i), handled elsewhere).

  THE NUMERIC GATE. `assert-scroll-range` scrolls the vertical scrollbar to
  its own `maximum()` - the number a real mouse wheel is capped by, per
  SStdMixerView::tkVerticalScrollMaximum() - and reports whether the true
  last row ends up fully visible. Deliberately not `set-lane-view
  topRow=<rowCount-1>`: that call clamps only to rowCount()-1 and bypasses
  the scrollbar's maximum() entirely, so it cannot see this bug at all.

  THE VIEWPORT SIZE is forced through `assert-lane-alignment`'s own
  `grabWidth`/`grabHeight` (the same resize-then-layout-activate trick
  `grabArrangerLanes` uses elsewhere), which is also a free alignment check
  on six equal-height lanes. The requested height and the resulting canvas
  height are NOT the same number (the surrounding chrome absorbs some of
  it), so the two viewport sizes below are chosen by MEASURED effect
  (`canvasHeight=` in the report) rather than by arithmetic on the request:
  a small height that leaves the six lanes overflowing it, and a height
  an order of magnitude larger than 600px of content so there is no doubt
  it fits however the chrome maps. The resize only happens when `grabPng`
  is given — an `assert-lane-alignment` with no grab args (as below, after
  the scroll) deliberately does NOT re-resize, so the geometry the scroll
  was measured against is not disturbed by checking it.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="track_list_scroll_padding">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <grid-disable/>

    <!-- Six equal 100px lanes (the default base track height) - total
         content 600px. -->
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-track index="-1"/>

    <!-- ===== THE BUG'S OWN SHAPE: overflow that is NOT an exact multiple ===
         A small requested height leaves a lane band well short of the six
         lanes' 600px (measured: canvasHeight=579, i.e. a 563px lane band -
         not a multiple of the 100px lane height, so the sixth row is only
         partially inside it before any fix). -->
    <assert-lane-alignment grabPng="scroll_pad_overflow.png"
                           grabWidth="900" grabHeight="546"/>

    <!-- There IS scroll room (the old maximum here was exactly 0), and
         scrolling to the scrollbar's own maximum brings the true last row
         fully into view - the whole point of the padding. -->
    <assert-scroll-range expectMaxAtLeast="1" expectFullyVisible="true"/>

    <!-- Heads still sit exactly on their lanes after the scroll - the
         padding is scroll range only and must not have disturbed the
         geometry every OTHER assertion here depends on. This does NOT
         re-resize (no grab args), so it checks the same geometry the scroll
         above was measured against. -->
    <assert-lane-alignment/>

    <!-- ===== THE NEGATIVE CONTROL: content that already fits =============
         A height an order of magnitude past 600px of content, so it fits
         comfortably however the surrounding chrome maps the request.
         Scrolling must stay DISABLED - the fix must not manufacture scroll
         room nobody needs, and the top row must not move.

         Reset the scroll to the top FIRST: visibleRowCountFrom() (and so
         verticalScrollMaximum()) measures "how many rows fit" from the
         CURRENT top row, and the run above left it scrolled to row 1 - from
         there, with a huge viewport, only 5 of the 6 rows are even
         countable, which would manufacture exactly the same maxScroll=1
         this control means to rule out, for a reason that has nothing to do
         with the fix under test. A resize (which recomputes the maximum)
         always follows a real top-row change in production, through the
         same recalcPageStep() this measures. -->
    <set-lane-view topRow="0"/>
    <assert-lane-alignment grabPng="scroll_pad_fits.png"
                           grabWidth="900" grabHeight="4000"/>
    <assert-scroll-range expectMaxAtMost="0" expectFullyVisible="true"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="6"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
